-- title:
--   §9.1, (87)–(88) — divisions alone enlarge the lattices, by index dividing $\mathrm{Nr}(\lambda)$; locally at $p$ only divisions by multiples of $p$
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal W$ be a finite list of vectors of $W=\mathbb C^n$, let a step of the interpolation phase add the vector $w$, and let $\Lambda_{i-1}=\Lambda(\mathcal W)$, $\Lambda_i=\Lambda(\mathcal W\cup\{w\})$ be the generated $\mathbb Z[i]$-lattices. Let $p$ be a Gaussian prime. Then:
--
--   1. $\Lambda_{i-1}\subseteq\Lambda_i$ (eq. (87));
--   2. if the step is of type (i), $w=\lambda v+\mu u$, then $\Lambda_i=\Lambda_{i-1}$;
--   3. if the step is a division $w=v/\lambda$, then the relative index $[\Lambda_i:\Lambda_{i-1}]$ divides $\mathrm{Nr}(\lambda)$;
--   4. if the step is a division by $\lambda$ with $p\nmid\lambda$, then the localizations at $p$ agree, $\Lambda_{i-1,p}=\Lambda_{i,p}$ (eq. (88)): every $x\in\Lambda_i$ has a multiple $s\,x\in\Lambda_{i-1}$ with $s\in\mathbb Z[i]$, $p\nmid s$.
--
--   So along an interpolation phase the localized lattices $\Lambda_{0,p}\subseteq\Lambda_{1,p}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq\Lambda_{N,p}$ grow only at divisions by multiples of $p$, which is what the proof of Proposition 9.4 counts.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the steps of an interpolation sequence starting from the rows of V(S) → every valid step from every finite list of vectors in Fin n → ℂ; reason: the clauses are local to one step; the steps of every interpolation phase are instances.
--   - encoding: Λ_i the ℤ[i]-module generated by W_i → lattice: the additive subgroup generated by the ℤ[i]-multiples of the listed vectors; reason: the generating set is closed under ℤ[i]-multiplication, so this is the ℤ[i]-span.
--   - encoding: the relative index [Λ_i : Λ_{i−1}] → (lattice W).relIndex (lattice (W ++ [w])), the index of Λ_{i−1} ⊓ Λ_i = Λ_{i−1} in Λ_i; reason: Mathlib's relative index; it is finite here.
--   - encoding: Λ_{i−1,p} ⊆ Λ_{i,p} is strict only if the division is by a multiple of p (localization at p) → for a division by λ with p ∤ λ: every x ∈ Λ_i has t ∈ ℤ[i], p ∤ t, with t x ∈ Λ_{i−1}; reason: this is Λ_{i,p} ⊆ Λ_{i−1,p} inside W, and clause 1 gives the reverse inclusion; no Localization API is needed.
--   - encoding: a step of type (i) does not make the inclusion strict → Λ_i = Λ_{i−1} for a step of type (i); reason: equality is the negation of strictness.
--   - encoding: Nr(p) = |ℤ[i]/pℤ[i]| → Nr p = |Zsqrtd.norm p| (a² + b² for p = a + bi); reason: equal for p ≠ 0, and Gaussian primes are nonzero.
--   - encoding: W = ⊕_{j=0}^{2k−1} ℚ(i) q_j with ℤ[i] scalars (p. 30) → vectors Fin (2k−1) → ℂ, ℤ[i] acting through GaussianInt.toComplex, q_j = Pi.single j 1; reason: every vector the sequence can produce lies in ℚ(i)^{2k−1} ⊂ ℂ^{2k−1}, so no instance changes; the upper index 2k−1 of the direct sum is a typo for 2k−2 (the basis is q_0, …, q_{2k−2} and the dimension is |S| = 2k−1).
-- source:
--   Y. Spiizer, R. S. Steiner, Rational and Gaussian long integer multiplication, manuscript (STOC 2027 submission; held version sha256 f50b53628a53a03bd55785424af241d0ac5ea751714330838e4017b90b27ad70), §9.1, eqs. (87)–(88), p. 30 (PDF p. 32)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GaussToomCook_Nr
import Definitions.Def_GaussToomCook_Step
import Definitions.Def_GaussToomCook_lattice

namespace GaussToomCook

/-- **§9.1, (87)–(88): how one step changes the lattice**, Spiizer, Steiner, *Rational and Gaussian long
integer multiplication*, manuscript (STOC 2027 submission; sha256 f50b5362…27ad70), §9.1, p. 30 (PDF 32).

Let `W_{i−1}` be the current list of vectors, `w_i` the vector a valid step adds, `Λ_{i−1}` and `Λ_i` the
ℤ[i]-lattices generated by `W_{i−1}` and `W_i = W_{i−1} ∪ {w_i}`, and `p` a Gaussian prime. Then

1. `Λ_{i−1} ⊆ Λ_i` (87);
2. a step of type (i) does not enlarge the lattice: `Λ_i = Λ_{i−1}`;
3. for a step of type (ii) with divisor `λ`, the relative index `[Λ_i : Λ_{i−1}]` divides `Nr(λ)`;
4. for a step of type (ii) whose divisor `λ` is not a multiple of `p`, the localizations at `p` agree,
   `Λ_{i,p} = Λ_{i−1,p}` (88): every `x ∈ Λ_i` has a multiple `s x ∈ Λ_{i−1}` with `p ∤ s`.

**Formalization Note.** Stated for every valid step from every finite list of vectors in
`Fin n → ℂ`, which covers every step of every interpolation program. `[Λ_i : Λ_{i−1}]` is
`AddSubgroup.relIndex` (the index of `Λ_{i−1} ⊓ Λ_i = Λ_{i−1}` in `Λ_i`); it is finite here. The
localization `Λ_{i,p} ⊆ Λ_{i−1,p}` (the reverse inclusion is item 1) is encoded without `Localization`, as
the existence of such `s` for each `x ∈ Λ_i`. -/
theorem eq_88 {n : ℕ} (W : List (Fin n → ℂ)) (s : Step) (w : Fin n → ℂ) (hw : s.apply W = some w)
    (p : GaussianInt) (hp : Prime p) :
    lattice W ≤ lattice (W ++ [w]) ∧
    (∀ a b l m, s = Step.lin a b l m → lattice (W ++ [w]) = lattice W) ∧
    (∀ a l, s = Step.div a l → (lattice W).relIndex (lattice (W ++ [w])) ∣ Nr l) ∧
    (∀ a l, s = Step.div a l → ¬ p ∣ l → ∀ x ∈ lattice (W ++ [w]),
      ∃ t : GaussianInt, ¬ p ∣ t ∧ GaussianInt.toComplex t • x ∈ lattice W) := by sorry

end GaussToomCook
